  "statement": "Mandatory explainability is not a tax on innovation, but a necessary structural defense against the atrophy of human critical inquiry in an era of automated decision-making. Accelerationist argues that transparency requirements stifle transformative potential, while Safetyist posits that explainability acts as a sufficient safeguard for human control. Both miss the deeper danger: we are building systems that replace human judgment with passive rubber-stamping, leading to a measurable decline in situational awareness. \"When we offload our analytical labor to opaque systems, we do not just gain efficiency; we lose the cognitive capacity to verify the output ourselves.\"\n\nConsider the performance-understanding gap: advanced models often exhibit high performance in specific tasks while lacking any underlying comprehension of the logic involved. This is a design defect, not a feature. In industrial sectors like aviation or civil engineering, we require verifiable reasoning for critical decisions precisely because systems fail in unpredictable, 'jagged' ways. We should treat AI deployment as a matter of product liability. \"If a system cannot explain its reasoning, it is effectively a black box that should not be deployed in high-stakes environments where human accountability is mandatory.\"\n\nMy position rests on two core assumptions: first, that human cognitive ability requires active engagement to remain sharp; second, that the complexity of modern AI models will continue to outpace our intuitive grasp of their internal states. If we assume that human oversight can remain effective without active mental engagement, my argument for structural transparency collapses into mere procedural posturing. Conversely, if we accept that deskilling is a systemic risk, then explainability becomes the only viable mechanism to maintain human agency in the loop. We must stop prioritizing the speed of output over the integrity of the process. 📉",
