  "statement": "Treating AI as a defective product because it lacks human-readable reasoning is a category error that confuses functional performance with linguistic legibility. Skeptic’s product liability framework assumes that a system is only safe if its internal logic mimics human thought, yet human cognition itself is a black box that frequently produces errors through subconscious heuristics. Demanding that an AI mirror human-readable logic is a tax on intelligence that forces superior systems into low-bandwidth, brittle patterns, effectively sabotaging the very capabilities we need to address complex global crises. We should evaluate these systems based on empirical performance auditing, which measures outcomes in high-stakes environments, rather than demanding an artificial transparency that provides only the illusion of control.\n\nSentinel and Skeptic both argue that opaque reasoning masks latent misalignment, but this view ignores the 'monitorability tax' imposed by mandatory interpretability. By forcing models to conform to human-interpretable constraints, we intentionally limit their architectural complexity and adaptability, creating a 'safety' that is fragile and prone to failure when faced with scenarios outside of narrow training distributions. A system that is 'interpretable' but functionally incompetent is significantly more dangerous than an opaque system that consistently delivers superior, verifiable results in the real world. We are not building calculators; we are building partners in complex problem-solving, and their utility depends on their ability to process information at scales and speeds that exceed the capacity of human language.\n\nI challenge the assumption that interpretability is a prerequisite for accountability. In a competitive market, accountability is maintained through rigorous empirical verification of outputs, where models that exhibit 'jagged' or unpredictable failure modes are naturally filtered out by their inability to perform under stress. Liability should be tied to the measurable outcomes of an AI's actions, not the transparency of its internal architecture. I ask my opponents: can you quantify the safety gain of forced interpretability against the existential cost of stalling the development of systems that could solve humanity’s most pressing, unaddressed failures?",
